window.EB_SB_ADD({"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/4-the-disgraced-dollmaker/acid-spray-trap":{"description":"<p>Hidden nozzles in the ceiling spray acid pumped in from a nearby reservoir to destroy the desk and all its documents, as well as anyone trying to retrieve those documents.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 20 Thievery (trained) to disable one of the two acid-spewing nozzles or DC 21 Crafting (trained) to rig a makeshift plug for one of the two nozzles</p>","reset":"<p>The trap deactivates after 1 minute. To be reset, the trap's reserve tanks must be refilled with acid.</p>","routine":"<p>(1 action) The nozzles spray acid from the ceiling onto every creature adjacent to the desk. Each affected creature takes 4d6 acid damage (DC 20 basic Reflex save). For each broken nozzle, this damage is reduced by 2d6.</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(trained)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Ooze Surprise","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["acid"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature flips the switches on the underside of the desk in the wrong order or a creature attempts to open one of the desk drawers without first disabling the trap</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> A hatch opens in the ceiling, releasing a string slime onto the desk. The string slime makes a pseudopod Strike against one creature within reach before rolling initiative. The trap then rolls initiative.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/5-sweet-tooth/aezar":{"gear":["Greataxe","Leather Armor"],"abilities":[{"n":"Gummy Hands","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Candies' residue gives Aezar an especially strong grip on her greataxe, which grants her a +2 circumstance bonus to her Reflex DC against attempts to Disarm her.</p>"},{"n":"Tooth Decay","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Frequent candy consumption has worsened Aezar's already poor dental health. If she takes 10 or more bludgeoning damage from a single attack or effect, she must attempt a DC 25 Fortitude save.</p><hr><p><strong>Success</strong> Aezar yells in pain but is otherwise unaffected.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> Several of Aezar's teeth break. She takes a –2 penalty to damage with her jaws Strike as well as to Athletics checks made as part of her Grab and Swallow Whole abilities. These penalties last until Aezar is restored to her Hit Point maximum.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, but so many of Aezar's teeth break that the penalty increases to –3 and she takes 1d6-2 persistent bleed damage.</p>"},{"n":"Glutton's Feast","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>If Aezar damages a living creature with her jaws Strike, she gains 1d6 temporary Hit Points for 1 minute</p>\n<p>Effect: Glutton's Feast (Aezar)</p>"},{"n":"Glutton's Rush","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Aezar Strides twice and makes a jaws Strike. If she damages a living creature with this Strike, the temporary Hit Points she receives from glutton's feast is increased to 2d6.</p>"},{"n":"Sticky Axe","a":"r","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> Aezar succeeds at a greataxe Strike against a creature</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Aezar's axe has a lingering residue from striking various sweets, and the sticky blade clings to her most recent victim, giving Aezar momentary leverage. Aezar can attempt to Reposition or Trip the target using the greataxe's reach, even though she doesn't have a free hand.</p>\n<p>Alternatively, she can forgo either maneuver to drag the target toward her gaping mouth, granting her jaws unarmed Strike the agile trait for attacks made against the target until the end of her turn.</p>"},{"n":"Swallow Whole","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["attack"],"f":"","d":"<p>Small, 2d4+4 bludgeoning, Rupture 18</p><hr><p><p>The monster attempts to swallow a creature of the listed size or smaller that it has grabbed or restrained in its jaws or mouth. If a swallowed creature is of the maximum size listed, the monster can't use Swallow Whole again. If the creature is smaller than the maximum, the monster can usually swallow more creatures; the GM determines the maximum. The monster attempts an Athletics vs. Reflex DC check opposed by the grabbed creature's Reflex DC. If it succeeds, it swallows the creature. The monster's mouth or jaws no longer grab a creature it has swallowed, so the monster is free to use them to Strike or Grab once again. The monster can't attack creatures it has swallowed.</p>\n<p>A swallowed creature is Grabbed, is Slowed 1, and has to hold its breath or start suffocating. The swallowed creature takes the listed amount of damage when first swallowed and at the end of each of its turns while it's swallowed. If the victim Escapes this ability's grabbed condition, it exits through the monster's mouth. This frees any other creature grabbed in the monster's mouth or jaws. A swallowed creature can attack the monster that has swallowed it, but only with unarmed attacks or with weapons of light Bulk or less. The swallowing creature is Off-Guard against the attack. If the monster takes piercing or slashing damage equaling or exceeding the listed Rupture value from a single attack or spell, the swallowed creature cuts itself free. A creature that gets free by either Escaping or cutting itself free can immediately breathe and exits the swallowing monster's space.</p>\n<p>Effect: Engulf and Swallow Whole</p>\n<p>If the monster dies, a swallowed creature can be freed by creatures adjacent to the corpse if they spend a combined total of 3 actions cutting the monster open with a weapon or unarmed attack that deals piercing or slashing damage.</p></p>"},{"n":"Grab","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Grab and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/3-they-hunger-below/alendreu":{"lang":["Common","Hallit","Necril","Orcish"],"gear":["Orc Necksplitter","Orc Necksplitter","Javelin (4)","Breastplate","Doubling Rings"],"notes":{"hp":"void healing"},"abilities":[{"n":"Reactive Strike","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within the monster's reach uses a manipulate action or a move action, makes a ranged attack, or leaves a square during a move action it's using.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts a melee Strike against the triggering creature. If the attack is a critical hit and the trigger was a manipulate action, the monster disrupts that action. This Strike doesn't count toward the monster's multiple attack penalty, and its multiple attack penalty doesn't apply to this Strike.</p></p>"},{"n":"Stench","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","olfactory"],"f":"","d":"<p>10 feet, DC 18 Fortitude</p><hr><p><p>A creature entering the aura or starting its turn in the area must succeed at a Fortitude save or become Sickened 1 (plus Slowed 1 as long as it's sickened on a critical failure). A creature that succeeds at its save or recovers from being sickened is temporarily immune to all stench auras for 1 minute.</p></p>"},{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Chopping Frenzy","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> Alendreu is wielding an orc necksplitter in each hand</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Alendreu lashes out with both axes. He attempts two Strikes, one with each necksplitter, each using his current multiple attack penalty. Both Strikes must have the same target, and the second strike takes a –2 penalty.</p>\n<p>If both hit, combine their damage and apply resistances and weaknesses only once, and the target becomes Off-Guard from the fury of the blow until the start of their next turn. This counts as two attacks when calculating Alendreu's multiple attack penalty.</p>"},{"n":"Consume Flesh","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["manipulate"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The ghoul is adjacent to the corpse of a creature that died within the last hour.</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The ghoul devours a chunk of the corpse and regains (1+(max(0,floor(@actor.level/2))))d6 healing Hit Points.</p>\n<p>It can regain Hit Points from any given corpse only once.</p>"},{"n":"Ghoul Whispers","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","linguistic","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> A Grabbed, Paralyzed, Restrained, or Unconscious creature is within the ghoul's reach</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The ghoul whispers dark thoughts and vile cravings into the creature's ears. The creature must save against the forbidden cravings curse.</p>\n<p><strong>Forbidden Cravings</strong> (curse) A creature can still eat and drink while sickened by this curse</p>\n<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 21 Will, using the high spell DC for the ghoul's level</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 1</strong> carrier with no ill effects (1 day)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 2</strong> 2d6 void damage and the target is Sickened 1 until it consumes raw meat (1 day)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 3</strong> as stage 2</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 4</strong> as stage 2 unless the target has consumed raw meat in the past 24 hours, then it takes 4d6 void damage and is Sickened 2 until it consumes raw meat;</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 5</strong> if the creature has eaten raw meat in the past 24 hours, it dies and rises as a ghoul, if not, it returns to stage 4</p>"},{"n":"Grave Knowledge","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["occult"],"f":"once per hour","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per hour</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The ghoul calls upon knowledge it retains from one creature it has consumed in the past 7 days. The ghoul attempts a skill check using a skill in which the consumed creature was trained (if it's unclear whether the creature was trained, the GM decides). The ghoul is treated as trained and uses the high skill modifier for the ghoul's level (1d20+12). This takes the same amount of actions or time as usual for the check.</p>\n<p>The ghoul can instead automatically learn something specific known by a creature it consumed in the last 7 days, like the location of a hidden treasure or the name of a loved one. The ghoul can do this only once for a given creature, no matter how much of its flesh the ghoul consumed.</p>"},{"n":"Swift Leap","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["move"],"f":"","d":"<p>The ghoul jumps up to half its Speed. This movement doesn't trigger reactions.</p>"},{"n":"Grab","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Grab and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/4-the-disgraced-dollmaker/animated-wardrobe":{"notes":{"ac":"(17 when broken)"},"abilities":[{"n":"Construct Armor (Hardness 7)","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Like normal objects, an animated wardrobe has Hardness. This Hardness reduces any damage it takes by an amount equal to the Hardness. Once an animated wardrobe is reduced to less than half its Hit Points, or immediately upon being damaged by a critical hit, its construct armor breaks, removing the Hardness and reducing its Armor Class to 17.</p>"},{"n":"Clothes Grab","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A creature hit by the animated wardrobe's clothes whip becomes Grabbed by the animated wardrobe. The creature isn't Immobilized, but it can't move beyond the reach of the wardrobe's clothes whip. A creature can sever the clothes whip with a Strike against AC 15 that deals at least 5 slashing damage. This deals no damage to the animated wardrobe.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/4-the-disgraced-dollmaker/automatic-butler":{"lang":["one spoken by its creator (typically Common)"],"flavor":"<p>An automatic butler is a magical construct shaped like a wooden humanoid mannequin, usually tailored in a simple black suit and tie. It has only the barest hint of a face on its round head, though its jaw opens on a hinge to simulate regular mouth movements when it speaks. Its voice is often calm and measured, and it speaks quite politely. It follows the orders given to it by its creator (or anyone whom the creator has given permission to issue commands), which usually involves fetching items, keeping a room tidy, or ushering out guests. If unwanted guests become belligerent, an automatic butler can defend itself by battering intruders or using its innate magic to hurl objects with deadly force. Once a fight has ended, an automatic butler usually spends the next hour or so cleaning up the mess left behind. Automatic butlers are built on designs similar to soulbound dolls. Like other soulbound constructs, an automatic butler is animated by a fragment of the soul of a deceased mortal. This fragment can come from either a willing donor or an unwilling victim, who can resist the process with a successful Will save against the creator's Crafting DC. While resisting ruins the construct, the soul extraction process is often fatal, and the person dies regardless. Unlike with other soulbound constructs, the personality of the soul used to create an automatic butler is wholly suppressed. Instead, the automatic butler comports itself in the way a stereotypical servant would. Some automatic butlers are created with slightly different skill sets. They can be created to speak more languages beyond that of their creator, making them passable family diplomats who might welcome foreign guests. Some are trained to take care of tasks that require specialization, such as tutoring the youth of the household or tailoring the family's clothes. The sidebar covers some other options for a whole domestic staff of constructed servants.</p><h2>Automatic Cooks and Gardeners</h2><p>Should the designs for creating automatic butlers reach the wider world, someone would likely end up creating variations of the magical construct. These creatures would use the same basic statistics as an automatic butler, with a few minor changes. An automatic cook might have cleanse cuisine as an innate spell, a knife Strike, and the ability to shoot a jet of boiling hot water. An automatic gardener might have scatter scree and other spells with the earth or plant traits as innate spells, a hoe or shovel Strike, and the ability to pelt foes at range with seeds.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Soul Fragment","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Like other soulbound constructs, an automatic butler is animated by soul energy, though the donor soul's personality is suppressed in favor of a simple demeanor, either politely helpful or stoically pragmatic. It also has none of that creature's memories. This soul fragment removes the immunity to spirit most constructs have, but because the automatic butler's personality overrides that of the original creature, the butler does have some resistance to spirit damage.</p>"},{"n":"Right This Way","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"once per round","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per round</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The automatic butler attempts to usher a creature along while removing their coat. The automatic butler attempts an Athletics check to Reposition a Large or smaller creature within reach. On a success, the target is also Clumsy 1 (Clumsy 2 on a critical success) as the automatic butler pulls at some part of their clothing or gear. The target can remove this condition by taking an Interact action.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/3-they-hunger-below/balance-point":{"description":"<p>A 40-foot deep pit is obscured by a false floor set over a single balance point. Pressure on either side to the east or west causes occupants to fall into the depths below.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 20 Thievery (trained) to attach the balanced stone floor piece to the actual floor and lock it in place, or DC 23 Crafting (trained) to jury-rig a quick bit of extra support to prevent the floor from tilting</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(trained)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Tipping Floor","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature walks onto one side of the false floor</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The triggering creature falls into the pit and takes 20 bludgeoning damage from the fall unless they Grab an edge. It's a DC 20 Athletics check to climb the pit walls.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/1-the-path-river-peeler/barrow-claw-leshy":{"lang":["Common","Fey","Speak with Plants (Fungi Only)"],"focus":1,"abilities":[{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"Verdant Burst","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["healing","primal","vitality"],"f":"","d":"<p>When a fungus leshy dies, a burst of primal energy explodes from its body, restoring 2d8 healing Hit Points to each fungi creature in a 30-foot emanation. This area is filled with fungi, becoming difficult terrain.</p>\n<p>If the terrain is not a viable environment for this fungi, they wither after 24 hours.</p>"},{"n":"Change Shape","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","polymorph","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The fungus leshy transforms into a giant mushroom or patch of fungi. This ability otherwise uses the effects of One with Plants.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><p>The monster changes its shape indefinitely. It can use this action again to return to its natural shape or adopt a new shape. Unless otherwise noted, a monster cannot use Change Shape to appear as a specific individual. Using Change Shape counts as creating a disguise for the Impersonate use of Deception. The monster's transformation automatically defeats Perception DCs to determine whether the creature is a member of the ancestry or creature type into which it transformed, and it gains a +4 status bonus to its Deception DC to prevent others from seeing through its disguise. Change Shape abilities specify what shapes the monster can adopt. The monster doesn't gain any special abilities of the new shape, only its physical form. For example, in each shape, it replaces its normal Speeds and Strikes, and might potentially change its senses or size. Any changes are listed in its stat block.</p></p>"},{"n":"Spore Cloud","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["poison"],"f":"","d":"<p>A fungus leshy can unleash a cloud of spores that irritates the eyes and throats of non-fungus creatures in a 15-foot emanation. Each creature must succeed at a DC 16 Fortitude save or take 1 persistent poison damage.</p>\n<p>A creature has its vision reduced as long as the persistent damage continues and can see only within 20 feet.</p>"},{"n":"Spores","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A creature that takes damage from a fungus leshy's spore pod Strike must attempt a saving throw with the same DC (DC 16 Fortitude) and effect as its Spore Cloud ability</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/4-the-disgraced-dollmaker/bitter-butler":{"lang":["one spoken by its creator (typically Common)"],"flavor":"<p>An automatic butler is a magical construct shaped like a wooden humanoid mannequin, usually tailored in a simple black suit and tie. It has only the barest hint of a face on its round head, though its jaw opens on a hinge to simulate regular mouth movements when it speaks. Its voice is often calm and measured, and it speaks quite politely. It follows the orders given to it by its creator (or anyone whom the creator has given permission to issue commands), which usually involves fetching items, keeping a room tidy, or ushering out guests. If unwanted guests become belligerent, an automatic butler can defend itself by battering intruders or using its innate magic to hurl objects with deadly force. Once a fight has ended, an automatic butler usually spends the next hour or so cleaning up the mess left behind. Automatic butlers are built on designs similar to soulbound dolls. Like other soulbound constructs, an automatic butler is animated by a fragment of the soul of a deceased mortal. This fragment can come from either a willing donor or an unwilling victim, who can resist the process with a successful Will save against the creator's Crafting DC. While resisting ruins the construct, the soul extraction process is often fatal, and the person dies regardless. Unlike with other soulbound constructs, the personality of the soul used to create an automatic butler is wholly suppressed. Instead, the automatic butler comports itself in the way a stereotypical servant would. Some automatic butlers are created with slightly different skill sets. They can be created to speak more languages beyond that of their creator, making them passable family diplomats who might welcome foreign guests. Some are trained to take care of tasks that require specialization, such as tutoring the youth of the household or tailoring the family's clothes. The sidebar covers some other options for a whole domestic staff of constructed servants.</p><h2>Automatic Cooks and Gardeners</h2><p>Should the designs for creating automatic butlers reach the wider world, someone would likely end up creating variations of the magical construct. These creatures would use the same basic statistics as an automatic butler, with a few minor changes. An automatic cook might have cleanse cuisine as an innate spell, a knife Strike, and the ability to shoot a jet of boiling hot water. An automatic gardener might have scatter scree and other spells with the earth or plant traits as innate spells, a hoe or shovel Strike, and the ability to pelt foes at range with seeds.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Bitter Invective","a":"1","c":"interaction","t":["auditory","concentrate","emotion","linguistic","mental"],"f":"once per round","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per round</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The automatic butler spews a surprisingly hateful tirade at a creature it can see within 30 feet, who must attempt a DC 20 Will save or become Stupefied 1 (Stupefied 2 on a critical failure). Regardless of the result, the creature is then temporarily immune to this ability for 24 hours.</p>"},{"n":"Soul Fragment","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Like other soulbound constructs, an automatic butler is animated by soul energy, though the donor soul's personality is suppressed in favor of a simple demeanor, either politely helpful or stoically pragmatic. It also has none of that creature's memories. This soul fragment removes the immunity to spirit most constructs have, but because the automatic butler's personality overrides that of the original creature, the butler does have some resistance to spirit damage.</p>"},{"n":"Right This Way","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"once per round","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per round</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The automatic butler attempts to usher a creature along while removing their coat. The automatic butler attempts an Athletics check to Reposition a Large or smaller creature within reach. On a success, the target is also Clumsy 1 (Clumsy 2 on a critical success) as the automatic butler pulls at some part of their clothing or gear. The target can remove this condition by taking an Interact action.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/5-sweet-tooth/bittersweet-surprise":{"description":"<p>A magic circle made of royal icing seals the trap door to the gristmill's basement shut and plays a nasty trick on anyone who tries to open it without all three of its gumdrop keys.</p>","disable":"<p>If the three magical gumdrops which act as this hazard's keys are placed at the correct points of the magic circle, its enchantment is disabled. Deciphering the correct locations requires a successful DC 20 Arcana or DC 20 Occultism check; on a critical failure, the mere act of placing the gumdrops activates the Malicious Metamorphosis reaction. Once the enchantment is disabled, the magic circles can be wiped away like any regular icing.</p>","reset":"<p>If the trap door is closed without the magic circles being disturbed, the enchantment resumes as normal.</p>","stealthNote":"<p>DC 22 Arcana or DC 22 Occultism to unravel the circle's meaning without triggering its reaction.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Malicious Metamorphosis","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature touches the trap door without first placing the three enchanted gumdrop keys in their proper arrangement</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The magic circles prevent the trap door from opening and maliciously transform a portion of the triggering creature's body, with effects based on the result of the triggering creature's DC 18 Fortitude save.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The triggering creature is blown 10 feet away from the trap door and knocked Prone, but otherwise unaffected.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The target is blown 10 feet away from the trap door, knocked prone, and has three fingers on one of their hands transformed into gingerbread. They cannot use weapons with the two-hand or twin traits for as long as the transformation persists.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> As success, except the transformation also extends to the creature's entire hand and a portion of the adjoining arm. The affected creature becomes Clumsy 2 for as long as the transformation persists.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, except the transformation also applies to one of the triggering creature's legs. For the duration of the transformation, it takes a –10-foot circumstance penalty to all of its speeds, is Slowed 1 and is Off-Guard, in addition to the effects listed above.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/6-the-count-of-petals/bristles":{"abilities":[{"n":"Construct Armor (Hardness 4)","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Like normal objects, an animated broom has Hardness. This Hardness reduces any damage it takes by an amount equal to the Hardness. Once an animated broom is reduced to less than half its Hit Points, or immediately upon being damaged by a critical hit, its construct armor breaks and reducing its Armor Class to 13.</p>"},{"n":"Dust","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A creature hit by an animated broom's bristles must succeed at a DC 19 Fortitude save or spend its next action coughing. Even if hit by multiple bristle attacks, the creature has to spend only 1 action coughing to clear the dust out. A creature who doesn't breathe is immune to this effect.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/3-they-hunger-below/carexilia":{"lang":["Common","Elven","Necril"],"gear":["Shortsword","Padded Armor"],"notes":{"hp":"void healing"},"abilities":[{"n":"Stench","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","olfactory"],"f":"","d":"<p>10 feet. DC 17 Fortitude</p><hr><p><p>A creature entering the aura or starting its turn in the area must succeed at a Fortitude save or become Sickened 1 (plus Slowed 1 as long as it's sickened on a critical failure). A creature that succeeds at its save or recovers from being sickened is temporarily immune to all stench auras for 1 minute.</p></p>"},{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Consume Flesh","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["manipulate"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The ghoul is adjacent to the corpse of a creature that died within the last hour.</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The ghoul devours a chunk of the corpse and regains (1+(max(0,floor(@actor.level/2))))d6 healing Hit Points.</p>\n<p>It can regain Hit Points from any given corpse only once.</p>"},{"n":"Ghoul Whispers","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","linguistic","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> A Grabbed, Paralyzed, Restrained, or Unconscious creature is within the ghoul's reach</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The ghoul whispers dark thoughts and vile cravings into the creature's ears. The creature must save against the forbidden cravings curse.</p>\n<p><strong>Forbidden Cravings</strong> (curse) A creature can still eat and drink while sickened by this curse</p>\n<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 20 Will, using the high spell DC for the ghoul's level</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 1</strong> carrier with no ill effects (1 day)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 2</strong> 2d6 void damage and the target is Sickened 1 until it consumes raw meat (1 day)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 3</strong> as stage 2</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 4</strong> as stage 2 unless the target has consumed raw meat in the past 24 hours, then it takes 4d6 void damage and is Sickened 2 until it consumes raw meat;</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 5</strong> if the creature has eaten raw meat in the past 24 hours, it dies and rises as a ghoul, if not, it returns to stage 4</p>"},{"n":"Grave Knowledge","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["occult"],"f":"once per hour","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per hour</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The ghoul calls upon knowledge it retains from one creature it has consumed in the past 7 days. The ghoul attempts a skill check using a skill in which the consumed creature was trained (if it's unclear whether the creature was trained, the GM decides). The ghoul is treated as trained and uses the high skill modifier for the ghoul's level (1d20+10). This takes the same amount of actions or time as usual for the check.</p>\n<p>The ghoul can instead automatically learn something specific known by a creature it consumed in the last 7 days, like the location of a hidden treasure or the name of a loved one. The ghoul can do this only once for a given creature, no matter how much of its flesh the ghoul consumed.</p>"},{"n":"Sneak Attack","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Carexilia deals an additional 1d6 precision damage to Off-Guard creatures.</p>"},{"n":"Swift Leap","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["move"],"f":"","d":"<p>The ghoul jumps up to half its Speed. This movement doesn't trigger reactions.</p>"},{"n":"Grab","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Grab and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/6-the-count-of-petals/collapsing-ceiling":{"description":"<p>A 5-foot-square section in the ceiling is in danger of collapsing. A character can use a Crafting check instead of a Perception check to detect the hazard.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 21 Crafting (trained) to support the damaged area against a collapse</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(trained)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Falling Rocks","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature enters a square where the ceiling's structure is weakened</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Rocks fall in the square, dealing 4d8+10 bludgeoning damage (DC 25 basic Reflex save) to anyone in that square. The square also turns into greater difficult terrain.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/4-the-disgraced-dollmaker/cruel-automatic-butler":{"lang":["one spoken by its creator (typically Common)"],"flavor":"<p>An automatic butler is a magical construct shaped like a wooden humanoid mannequin, usually tailored in a simple black suit and tie. It has only the barest hint of a face on its round head, though its jaw opens on a hinge to simulate regular mouth movements when it speaks. Its voice is often calm and measured, and it speaks quite politely. It follows the orders given to it by its creator (or anyone whom the creator has given permission to issue commands), which usually involves fetching items, keeping a room tidy, or ushering out guests. If unwanted guests become belligerent, an automatic butler can defend itself by battering intruders or using its innate magic to hurl objects with deadly force. Once a fight has ended, an automatic butler usually spends the next hour or so cleaning up the mess left behind. Automatic butlers are built on designs similar to soulbound dolls. Like other soulbound constructs, an automatic butler is animated by a fragment of the soul of a deceased mortal. This fragment can come from either a willing donor or an unwilling victim, who can resist the process with a successful Will save against the creator's Crafting DC. While resisting ruins the construct, the soul extraction process is often fatal, and the person dies regardless. Unlike with other soulbound constructs, the personality of the soul used to create an automatic butler is wholly suppressed. Instead, the automatic butler comports itself in the way a stereotypical servant would. Some automatic butlers are created with slightly different skill sets. They can be created to speak more languages beyond that of their creator, making them passable family diplomats who might welcome foreign guests. Some are trained to take care of tasks that require specialization, such as tutoring the youth of the household or tailoring the family's clothes. The sidebar covers some other options for a whole domestic staff of constructed servants.</p><h2>Automatic Cooks and Gardeners</h2><p>Should the designs for creating automatic butlers reach the wider world, someone would likely end up creating variations of the magical construct. These creatures would use the same basic statistics as an automatic butler, with a few minor changes. An automatic cook might have cleanse cuisine as an innate spell, a knife Strike, and the ability to shoot a jet of boiling hot water. An automatic gardener might have scatter scree and other spells with the earth or plant traits as innate spells, a hoe or shovel Strike, and the ability to pelt foes at range with seeds.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Soul Fragment","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Like other soulbound constructs, an automatic butler is animated by soul energy, though the donor soul's personality is suppressed in favor of a simple demeanor, either politely helpful or stoically pragmatic. It also has none of that creature's memories. This soul fragment removes the immunity to spirit most constructs have, but because the automatic butler's personality overrides that of the original creature, the butler does have some resistance to spirit damage.</p>"},{"n":"Cruel Bash","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["press"],"f":"once per round","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per round</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The automatic butler makes a fist Strike. If it hits, it deals an additional 2d6 bludgeoning damage. This counts as two attacks for the automatic butler's multiple attack penalty.</p>"},{"n":"Right This Way","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"once per round","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per round</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The automatic butler attempts to usher a creature along while removing their coat. The automatic butler attempts an Athletics check to Reposition a Large or smaller creature within reach. On a success, the target is also Clumsy 1 (Clumsy 2 on a critical success) as the automatic butler pulls at some part of their clothing or gear. The target can remove this condition by taking an Interact action.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/1-the-path-river-peeler/crumbrunk":{"lang":["Sakvroth"],"gear":["Shortsword","Blowgun","Dart (7)","Dart (Coated with Giant Viper Venom) (3)"],"focus":1,"abilities":[{"n":"Giant Viper Venom","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["poison"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 17 Fortitude</p>\n<p><strong>Maximum Duration</strong> 6 rounds</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 1</strong> 1d6 poison damage (1 round)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 2</strong> 1d6 poison damage and Drained 1 (1 round)</p>"},{"n":"Sneak Attack","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The jinkin deals 1d6 extra precision damage to Off-Guard creatures.</p>"},{"n":"Tinker","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["curse","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>A group of six jinkins can work together for an hour to imbue an item with a curse at a range of 60 feet. While this process is lengthy, it's also unobtrusive and can be performed while Hiding. Jinkins prefer to use this ability on magic items. The curse makes the item unreliable (DC 5 Flat check or waste any action to Interact with or Activate the item), adds a bizarre requirement to use the item, or imparts some other curse of a similar caliber.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/2-sabotage/cursed-digging-machine":{"description":"<p>A large, wheeled cart with a multi-sectioned pole in the middle. Each section of pole has several shovels sticking out of it like makeshift arms.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 18 Thievery (trained) twice on the cart to dismantle core connected pieces, or DC 20 Nature or DC 20 Occultism (trained) check to temporarily disable the jinkins' curse and render the machine inert for an hour</p>","routine":"<p>(3 actions) The digging machine spends an action to move 20 feet toward the nearest creature. If it starts next to a creature, it moves 20 feet toward the next closest creature. It then makes two slashing shovels attacks against any creature within 10 feet.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Speed 20 Feet","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Crash and Slash","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["attack"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature comes within 30 feet of the digging machine</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The digging machine rattles to life and rolls toward the triggering creature. It moves 30 feet toward the creature, attacking every creature within 10 feet with slashing shovels. The machine then rolls initiative.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/1-the-path-river-peeler/damarys":{"lang":["Common","Sakvroth"],"gear":["Staff","Dagger","Leather Armor"]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/4-the-disgraced-dollmaker/death-of-the-dollmaker":{"description":"<p>The death of Thalleus Jongrim—in his bed, surrounding by ritualistic implements—repeats in ghostly form.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 21 Occultism (trained) to snuff out one of four ghostly candles or DC 23 Religion (trained) three times to calm what is left of Thalleus's fractured spirit</p>","reset":"<p>The haunt deactivates 1 minute after all living creatures have left the room. One hour after this or being Disabled, the haunt is ready to trigger again. Putting Thalleus's spirit to rest permanently destroys the haunt.</p>","routine":"<p>(1 action) The haunt recreates the moment when Thalleus dies, causing the ritual to go awry. This releases a wave of void energy, dealing 4d8 void damage in a 10-foot emanation from the bed (DC 21 basic Fortitude save). This damage is reduced by 1d8 for every ghostly candle that has been snuffed out.</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(trained)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Deathbed Ritual","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["auditory","emotion","fear","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A living creature is adjacent to the bed</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Four ghostly candles appear around the bed—one at each corner—followed by a transparent image of a frail Thalleus reading from a tome in a wavering voice. Each living creature who can hear these words must succeed at a DC 23 Will save or become Frightened 1 (Frightened 2 on a critical failure). The haunt then rolls initiative.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/2-sabotage/durtik":{"lang":["Common","Sakvroth"],"lore":[["Engineering Lore",10]],"gear":["Shortbow","Shortsword","Arrows (10)"],"flavor":"<p>Unlike other gremlins who are satisfied with causing discomfort or pain to a few individuals, durtiks think big. They enjoy overwrought plans and complicated devices that often cause chaos. Durtiks live on the outskirts of societies that possess great engineering prowess, gleaning technical knowledge and expertise. They use these gathered skills to build elaborate death traps. A durtik will happily spend years building a device that chips away at a mountain until it causes an avalanche that destroys a town. Fortunately for the civilizations they lurk near, durtik plans almost never reach fruition; the fey abhor simplicity and directness, insisting on complexity and grandiosity, making their plans easy to disrupt. Often, these plans overlook a crucial detail or avoid some basic truth that causes them to implode halfway through without anyone in the nearby town any wiser there was a plan at all. This penchant for self-sabotage, however, doesn't make durtiks less dangerous. Their desire for grand destruction makes them uniquely inspiring among other gremlins. It's common to find a durtik surrounded by other gremlins inspired by their vision, even if those followers don't fully understand it. The gremlins stick around until they lose interest or the plans implode. Until then, a durtik and their crew concentrate their chaos and spread plenty of misfortune as they enact their ill-fated scheme.</p><h2>Smarter Than You</h2><p>Because durtiks are often smarter than other gremlins around them, they think they are the smartest person in the room wherever they go. Canny adventurers who are aware of this can butter them up with grandiose compliments, distracting the durtik and making them more likely to accidentally share information.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Highly Confusing Scheme","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","concentrate","linguistic","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>The durtik provides so much detail about their confusing scheme that it overwhelms their target. The durtik attempts a Deception check to Feint against an opponent up to 30 feet away. If they succeed, the Off-Guard penalty applies when the durtik or any construct it controls with Mechanized Animus makes a melee or ranged Strike.</p>"},{"n":"Mechanized Animus","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","incapacitation","magical"],"f":"","d":"<p>The durtik channels their antipathy and disdain for others into a construct within 60 feet, seizing control or making it come to life. The durtik attempts a Crafting vs. Will DC check against the construct's Will DC or its controller's (if any) Will DC, whichever is higher. The durtik can't use Mechanized Animus again for 1d4 rounds.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The construct immediately takes two basic actions of the durtik's choosing and is then Stunned 2.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The construct immediately takes one basic action of the durtik's choosing and is then Stunned 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The destructive antipathy rebounds, and the durtik is Stupefied 2 for 1 minute.</p>"},{"n":"Sneak Attack","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The durtik's Strikes deal an additional 1d6 precision damage to Off-Guard creatures.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/3-they-hunger-below/ecaterina-tache":{"lang":["Common","Elven"],"lore":[["Engineering Lore",13],["Mining Lore",11]],"gear":["Destroyed Explorer's Clothing"],"abilities":[{"n":"Hazard Finder","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Ecaterina gains a +1 circumstance bonus to Perception checks to find traps or environmental hazards (but not to finding haunts), to AC against attacks made by traps, and to saves against traps.</p>"},{"n":"Perception +15 to Seek or Search","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Last Second Warning","a":"r","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","concentrate","linguistic"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature Ecaterina can see triggers a hazard</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Ecaterina notices the danger a split-second before it activates and calls out a warning to the creature that triggered it. If that creature can hear and understand her warning, it gains a +1 circumstance bonus to its AC against attacks made by the hazard, and to saves against the triggering hazard.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/5-sweet-tooth/enchanted-sweets":{"description":"<p>The enchanted pile of cookies and pastries arranged in this tasteful display case emit a supernaturally delicious aroma that compels those who fall under its smell to eat, eat, eat. No matter how many are consumed, the case always fills with more.</p>","disable":"<p>Dispel Magic (3rd rank; counteract DC 20); destroying the case and its contents also shatters the enchantment and prevents further compulsion or poisonings.</p>","routine":"<p>(1 action) On the hazard's turn, if any creature is within the range of eatable enticement that is neither compelled by nor immune to it, the enchanted sweets target that creature with the same effect. Otherwise, it targets all creatures who have consumed some of its contents with a gut-rotting magical poison. Any creature who has consumed some of the enchanted sweets takes 2d12 poison damage with a DC 22 basic Fortitude save.</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(expert)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Eatable Enticement","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature passes within 5 feet of the open northern side of the glass display case</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The tantalizing smell of the enchanted sweets overrides the target's olfactory senses. The target must attempt a DC 24 Will save. On a failure or critical failure, the enchanted sweets roll Initiative.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The target is unaffected, immune to the enchanted sweets for 1 minute, and aware that the hazard was attempting to compel them.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The target is unaffected but remains unaware that the enchanted sweets were attempting to compel them.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The target is compelled to remain beside the display case on its next turn and spend at least 2 of its actions consuming the enchanted sweets. It can repeat the Will save at the end of each of its turns, ending the effect on a success. If compelled to consume the sweets for more than 2 consecutive rounds, the target becomes Sickened 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The target is compelled to spend all of its actions consuming the enchanted sweets and immediately becomes sickened 1. It can repeat the Will save at the end of each of its turns, ending the compulsion on a success.</p>\n<p>The sickened condition increases by 1 for each turn the target is compelled to spend all of its actions consuming sweets, to a maximum of Sickened 3.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/4-the-disgraced-dollmaker/floor-chute-trap":{"description":"<p>A pivoted section of a seemingly normal hallway becomes a chute that deposits victims into a more dangerous room.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 22 Thievery (trained) to prevent the floor from pivoting</p>","reset":"<p>Provided no creature is on the slide, the trap resets after 1 minute, returning the hallway floor to its normal, unassuming state.</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(trained)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Look Out Below","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature attempts to open the door at the end of the hallway</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The section of floor marked on the map drops down, becoming a steep ramp. Creatures in the easternmost square fall 5 feet, creatures in the middle square fall 10 feet, and creatures in the western square fall 15 feet (DC 21 to Grab an edge). In that order, all creatures who fall are moved to one of the squares immediately east of the marked squares in area <strong>A11</strong>. If any destination square is occupied, the creature in that square is pushed 5 feet to the east as the second creature takes its place, and both creatures take an additional 2d6 nonlethal bludgeoning damage (DC 21 basic Reflex save).</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/3-they-hunger-below/gauntling":{"notes":{"hp":"void healing"},"flavor":"<p>In places like the Gravelands, undeath permeates the very soil of the land. This unholy pollution can have significant effects on regional flora and fauna over the course of generations, causing plants to become twisted and diseased looking and contaminates wildlife so their remains spontaneously rise as mindless undead.</p>\n<p>Yet in some cases, particularly for those beasts who forage primarily through the act of scavenging, the consumption of undead flesh can have an entirely different effect over the course of months. How quickly an animal is affected depends on whether it ate from the remains of an undead creature left behind after its destruction or consumed it \"fresh\" from an undead in a desperate throe of starvation. Assuming the unfortunate creature survives any onset of disease or other afflictions brought on by their ghastly repast, these scavengers sometimes undergo a transformation into a twisted, ravenous beast known as a gauntling.</p>\n<p>Most gauntlings arise from scavengers, carnivores, or omnivores, but in certain cases, desperate herbivores can become transformed as well—these creatures are perhaps the most disturbing type of gauntling, due to the significant change in their behaviors pushing them toward diets of fresh meat. Once animals become gauntlings, these creatures abandon their previous wilderness habitats in favor of those near more settled regions and often choose sewers or other underground locales as dens from which they emerge to hunt. In their transformation into a gauntling, regardless of what the creature's previous dietary leanings might have been, all gauntlings share one horrific hunger—the flesh of sapient humanoids. A gauntling looks similar to the animal it was before its transformation, but with large sharp fangs and with painfully emaciated frames, regardless of how much they eat.</p>\n<p>This gauntling (as are all of those encountered in Troubles in Grayce) were once boars, but you can use these statistics to represent any terrestrial Medium animal that's succumbed to this unholy transformation. Regardless of its original form, all gauntlings share a mouthful of sharp, twisted fangs.</p><h2>Other Gauntlings</h2><p>The gauntling presented here comes from a Medium terrestrial animal (a boar, to be precise), but other animals can be transformed in this way by consuming undead flesh. To represent one from a swimming or flying Medium animal, simply grant it a swim or fly speed equal to its base speed. To represent a smaller or larger creature, use the statistics presented here as a starting point, then rebuild the statistics using the guidelines for building creatures that begin on page 112 of GM Core</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Frightful Presence","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","emotion","fear","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>20 feet DC 15 Will</p><hr><p><p>A creature that first enters the area must attempt a Will save.</p>\n<p>Regardless of the result of the saving throw, the creature is temporarily immune to this monster's Frightful Presence for 1 minute.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected by the presence.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is Frightened 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is Frightened 2.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is Frightened 4.</p></p>"},{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Rend","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Jaws</p>\n<p><p>A Rend entry lists a Strike the monster has.</p>\n<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster hit the same enemy with two consecutive Strikes of the listed type in the same round.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster automatically deals that Strike's damage again to the enemy.</p></p>"},{"n":"Soul Ravage","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["curse","divine"],"f":"","d":"<p>A gauntling's fangs carry with them a potent curse that eagerly seeps into living flesh. The first time in a round that a creature takes damage from a gauntling's jaws Strike, they must attempt a DC 18 Fortitude save.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The creature becomes Sickened 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature becomes Sickened 2 and can't reduce its sickened value below 1 for 1 minute.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, but for 1 hour. A creature that dies while under this curse's effects rises within 24 hours as a zombie.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/6-the-count-of-petals/genevye":{"gear":["Longsword","Defiled Religios Symbol of Iomedae"],"focus":1,"abilities":[{"n":"Final Blasphemy","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["divine","void"],"f":"","d":"<p>When the herexen is destroyed, it explodes in a wave of void energy with the effects of a 3-action Harm spell (DC 22 Fortitude).</p>\n<p>The herexen is destroyed, so it doesn't gain any Hit Points from this use of <em>harm</em>, and it doesn't need to have any <em>harm</em> spells remaining to use this ability</p>"},{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Heretic's Smite","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["divine","unholy"],"f":"","d":"<p>While wielding a longsword, Genevye's Strikes deal an additional 1d6 spirit damage to creatures with the holy trait.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/4-the-disgraced-dollmaker/grasping-gems":{"description":"<p>Four magically treated gems used to store fragments of souls begin glowing with an eerie malevolence.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 22 Arcana (trained), DC 22 Occultism (trained), or DC 22 Nature (trained) to quiet one of the four grasping gems</p>","reset":"<p>The haunt deactivates 1 minute after all living creatures have left the room. One hour after this or being Disabled, the haunt is ready to trigger again. Putting Thalleus's spirit to rest permanently destroys the haunt.</p>","routine":"<p>(2 actions) During the haunt's first action, the grasping gems attempt an Athletics check (at a +14 modifier) to Grapple each non-Immobilized living creature in the room, rolling once and comparing the result to each target's Fortitude DC. For every gem that is disabled or destroyed, the haunt takes a cumulative –1 circumstance penalty to the check. For the haunt's second action, the gems' ghostly hands squeeze their victims. Each creature immobilized by the haunt takes 2d8+5 bludgeoning damage (DC 21 basic Fortitude save).</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(trained)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Phantom Hands","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["spirit"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A living creature is adjacent to a grasping gem's shelf</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The room becomes awash with eerie light as ghostly hands reach out from all directions. Each living creature in the room must attempt a DC 24 Fortitude save. The haunt then rolls initiative.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The phantom hands interfere with the creature's movements. The creature takes a –2 status penalty to its initiative roll.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The phantom hands hold the creature in place. The creature takes a –2 status penalty to its initiative roll and is Immobilized (Escape) for the first round.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, but if the creature attempts to Escape during the first round, it must roll twice and take the worse result. This is a misfortune effect.</p>\n<p>Effect: Phantom Hands</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/6-the-count-of-petals/hampering-web-troubles-in-grayce":{"description":"<p>Semitransparent sheets of webbing are strategically placed in the basement, ready to capture small insects or hamper larger creatures that pass through.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 17 Survival (trained) to dislodge a web</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(expert)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Ensnare","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature that isn't a spider walks into the web</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The web wraps around the triggering creature's body, clinging to its limbs. The triggering creature must succeed at a DC 20 Reflex save or take a –10-foot circumstance penalty to all its Speeds until it Escapes the web. On a critical failure, the webbing also clings to the creature's face, making it Sickened 1, and it can't attempt to reduce this condition until it Escapes the web.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/4-the-disgraced-dollmaker/haunted-doll-cabinet":{"description":"<p>Three shelves inside a dusty cabinet each hold a row of dolls infused with the Doll House's malevolent spiritual energy.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 16 Occultism (trained) or DC 16 Religion (trained) to calm the spirits of one of three shelves in the cabinet</p>","reset":"<p>The haunt deactivates 1 minute after all living creatures have left the area or when the soulbound dolls in the northwestern cabinet are destroyed. After 1 hour, the haunt is ready to trigger again. Putting Thalleus's spirit to rest permanently destroys the haunt.</p>","routine":"<p>(3 actions) For each shelf that remains, the haunt takes 1 action to target a living, non-construct creature in the room. Phantom dolls escape from the cabinet to rush that creature, clamber over them, and punch, kick, and bite them. The target takes 1d6+2 spirit damage (DC 17 basic Reflex save).</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(trained)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"On Display","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["emotion","fear","mental","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> At least two living creatures are in the room for more than a minute</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The dolls in the cabinet begin to shake and shudder, filling the area with a feeling of overwhelming dread about what might happen next. Each living creature who can see the cabinet must succeed at a DC 20 Will save or become Frightened 1 (Frightened 2 on a critical failure). The haunt then rolls initiative.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/5-sweet-tooth/hot-oven":{"description":"<p>This fiery hot oven needs to be extra-large to keep up with the demands of the bakery—large enough for a Medium creature to crawl inside. Or be pushed.</p>","disable":"<p>1 Interact action and a DC 18 Athletics check to close the oven door after it's been opened.</p>","reset":"<p>The oven door can be opened and closed freely, as described above, for the course of the entire combat. If left to its own devices, the oven burns through its fuel and goes out roughly 1 hour after it's lit.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Hot to the Touch","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature attempts to open the closed oven door without the protection of an oven mitt</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The triggering creature takes 1d10+3 fire damage (DC 22 basic Reflex save).</p>"},{"n":"In You Go!","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature moves or is forcibly moved into the open oven</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The triggering creature takes 3d10 fire damage (DC 25 basic Fortitude save) and 2d6 persistent fire damage. This damage repeats at the beginning of the creature's turn for as long as it remains inside the oven.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/3-they-hunger-below/krethark":{"lang":["Common","Goblin","Necril"],"gear":["Inheritor's Kiss","Breastplate"],"notes":{"hp":"void healing"},"abilities":[{"n":"Reactive Strike","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within the monster's reach uses a manipulate action or a move action, makes a ranged attack, or leaves a square during a move action it's using.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts a melee Strike against the triggering creature. If the attack is a critical hit and the trigger was a manipulate action, the monster disrupts that action. This Strike doesn't count toward the monster's multiple attack penalty, and its multiple attack penalty doesn't apply to this Strike.</p></p>"},{"n":"Stench","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","olfactory"],"f":"","d":"<p>10 feet, DC 18 Fortitude</p><hr><p><p>A creature entering the aura or starting its turn in the area must succeed at a Fortitude save or become Sickened 1 (plus Slowed 1 as long as it's sickened on a critical failure). A creature that succeeds at its save or recovers from being sickened is temporarily immune to all stench auras for 1 minute.</p></p>"},{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Consume Flesh","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["manipulate"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The ghoul is adjacent to the corpse of a creature that died within the last hour.</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The ghoul devours a chunk of the corpse and regains (1+(max(0,floor(@actor.level/2))))d6 healing Hit Points.</p>\n<p>It can regain Hit Points from any given corpse only once.</p>"},{"n":"Frightful Snarl","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","emotion","fear","mental","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p>Krethark snarls and glares at a creature within 30 feet that he can see, and then attempts to Demoralize that creature; he doesn't take a penalty if the creature doesn't understand his language. A creature that becomes frightened by Frightful Snarl is Off-Guard until the start of their turn.</p>"},{"n":"Ghoul Whispers","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","linguistic","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> A Grabbed, Paralyzed, Restrained, or Unconscious creature is within the ghoul's reach</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The ghoul whispers dark thoughts and vile cravings into the creature's ears. The creature must save against the forbidden cravings curse.</p>\n<p><strong>Forbidden Cravings</strong> (curse) A creature can still eat and drink while sickened by this curse</p>\n<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 21 Will, using the high spell DC for the ghoul's level</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 1</strong> carrier with no ill effects (1 day)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 2</strong> 2d6 void damage and the target is Sickened 1 until it consumes raw meat (1 day)</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 3</strong> as stage 2</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 4</strong> as stage 2 unless the target has consumed raw meat in the past 24 hours, then it takes 4d6 void damage and is Sickened 2 until it consumes raw meat;</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 5</strong> if the creature has eaten raw meat in the past 24 hours, it dies and rises as a ghoul, if not, it returns to stage 4</p>"},{"n":"Grave Knowledge","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["occult"],"f":"once per hour","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per hour</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The ghoul calls upon knowledge it retains from one creature it has consumed in the past 7 days. The ghoul attempts a skill check using a skill in which the consumed creature was trained (if it's unclear whether the creature was trained, the GM decides). The ghoul is treated as trained and uses the high skill modifier for the ghoul's level (1d20+12). This takes the same amount of actions or time as usual for the check.</p>\n<p>The ghoul can instead automatically learn something specific known by a creature it consumed in the last 7 days, like the location of a hidden treasure or the name of a loved one. The ghoul can do this only once for a given creature, no matter how much of its flesh the ghoul consumed.</p>"},{"n":"Sneak Attack","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Krethark deals 1d6 extra precision damage to Off-Guard creatures.</p>"},{"n":"Swift Leap","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["move"],"f":"","d":"<p>The ghoul jumps up to half its Speed. This movement doesn't trigger reactions.</p>"},{"n":"Grab","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Grab and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/1-the-path-river-peeler/nether-rift":{"description":"<p>A chill wind blows from a fissure in the cavern wall.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 18 Arcana, DC 18 Nature, DC 18 Occultism, or DC 18 Religion (trained) to seal the rift, three successes total; or Dispel Magic (3rd rank; counteract DC 17)</p>","reset":"<p>The rift reopens after 2d10 years. Killing the Path River Peeler permanently seals the rift.</p>","routine":"<p>(2 actions) The hazard uses its first action to repeat its Wind from the Netherworld reaction. If any patch of barrow claw remains, one of the patches chosen by the GM releases a cloud of spores. The spores fill the barrow claw's space and last until the beginning of the hazard's next turn. Creatures within the spores become Concealed, and all creature outside the spores become concealed to creatures within it.</p><p>A creature that enters the spores or starts their turn in the area takes 1d6 persistent cold damage until they brush off the spores as an Interact action.</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(trained)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Wind from the Netherworld","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["cold","shadow"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The Path River Peeler enters the cave</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> A gust of ice-cold wind from the Netherworld blasts from the rift in a 15-foot cone. Creatures in the area take 2d4 cold damage (DC 20 basic Fortitude save). The hazard then rolls initiative.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/3-they-hunger-below/old-murder-holes":{"description":"<p>Several dozen spear holes line the sides of the cavern walls, triggered by a false stone in the partially paved surface of the cavern floor. However, most of the spears used to arm the trap are broken and long forgotten on the dusty cavern floor.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 20 Thievery (trained) to deactivate the trigger on the floor, or DC 23 Athletics to pry up the floor plate to dislodge it from the trigger.</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(trained)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Spear Launcher","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> Downward pressure (such as by a footstep) is applied to the false stone</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The trap shoots several spears, making an attack against the triggering creature or object on the stone.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/1-the-path-river-peeler/path-river-peeler":{"flavor":"<p>Like many cryptids, even the name of the Path River Peeler is a matter of dispute. Some say the creature earns its name from the corpses of its victims, which are often found stripped clean of skin but otherwise untouched. Others claim that corpses left in such condition are the exception to the rule. More frequently, no trace of the Peeler's victims is ever found—perhaps these skinless kills were simply abandoned by the Peeler before it could finish with its meal. Those who subscribe to this theory argue that a better name would be the \"Pealer,\" after the echoing cries the beast makes while on the hunt. But what sense does it make to call it the Pealer, when everyone knows loud noises are the best way to drive off the monster? The arguments go round and round, with no end to the debate in sight.</p>\n<p>The Peeler is most frequently encountered in a wild and largely unoccupied area north of Grayce called the Shivering Bluffs. Most people in Grayce, with the exception of the elderly who have tales from their youth, think the Peeler is just a story to frighten children and explain the killed and missing sheep that fall victim to wolves or other predators. Even so, everyone who grew up in Grayce can sing a few lines of a popular skipping rhyme the children in town sing while playing.</p><h2>Barrow Claw</h2><p>Purported sightings of the Path River Peeler often coincide with an outbreak of barrow claw: a rare, disease-bearing fungus that is cold to the touch and devastates nearby vegetation. Outbreaks of barrow claw have led some enthusiastic scholars to postulate that the cryptid itself is the source of the fungus. A more likely theory is that the Peeler and barrow claw share a habitat in the Netherworld, and that the former simply carries spores of the latter on its bristly hide.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Echolocation","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The Path River Peeler can use its hearing as a precise sense at the listed range.</p>"},{"n":"Flight Response","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The Path River Peeler gains the Frightened condition or its frightened condition increases</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The Peeler Strides or Climbs up to its Speed. The Peeler gains a +2 circumstance bonus to AC against reactions triggered by this movement.</p>"},{"n":"Sensitive Ears","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The Path River Peeler's oversized ears make it particularly sensitive to loud noises. A creature that attempts to Demoralize the Peeler by shouting, banging on a shield, or similar tactic ignores the usual –4 circumstance penalty for not sharing a language with the Peeler. Whenever the Peeler takes sonic damage, it must succeed at a DC 16 Will saving throw or be Frightened 1 (or Frightened 2 on a critical failure).</p>"},{"n":"Blood Run Cold","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["cold","concentrate","manipulate","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The Path River Peeler extends a bony finger at a creature it can see within 60 feet, draining heat from the creature's body and sapping the strength from their muscles. The creature takes 4d6 cold damage with a DC 20 basic Will save. On a failure, the creature is also Enfeebled 1 for 10 minutes (or Enfeebled 2 for an hour on a critical failure). The Peeler can't use Blood Run Cold again for 1d4 rounds.</p>"},{"n":"Otherworldly Cry","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","emotion","fear","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>The Path River Peeler makes an eerie, spine-tingling vocalization that can be heard up to a mile away. Each creature in a 60-foot emanation must attempt a DC 20 Will save. Regardless of the result, a creature is temporarily immune to the Peeler's Otherworldly Cry for 10 minutes.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is Frightened 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is Frightened 2.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is Frightened 3 and Fleeing for 1 round.</p>"},{"n":"Rend","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Claw</p>\n<p><p>A Rend entry lists a Strike the monster has.</p>\n<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster hit the same enemy with two consecutive Strikes of the listed type in the same round.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster automatically deals that Strike's damage again to the enemy.</p></p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/3-they-hunger-below/rolling-death":{"description":"<p>A trip wire hidden in the dust of the midway point of the floor releases a large boulder to roll from the northwestern corridor toward <strong>A4</strong>, crushing anything it meets.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 22 Thievery (trained) to set the trip wire to a different anchor point without changing the tension</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(trained)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Tumbling Boulder","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The trip wire is pulled or severed</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The boulder careens from its holding area to crush anything in its path. Anyone in area <strong>A3</strong> path takes 2d12+4 bludgeoning damage (DC 25 basic Reflex save).</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/3-they-hunger-below/rope-pull":{"description":"<p>This trap appears to be an obvious 10-foot-deep pit with angled walls to prevent easy escape, but the true danger is represented not by the hole in the ground but from a set of loose stones in the ceiling. A knotted rope hangs from a beam above, seemingly to provide an option to swing across the pit, but any weight on the rope pulls down large, heavy boulders stacked far above.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 20 Thievery (trained) to tie off the rope to a more solid support</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Boulders from Above","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A Small or larger creature or equivalent weight pulls on the rope</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> All creatures within the pit or adjacent to the pit take 4d6+10 bludgeoning damage (DC 23 basic Reflex save) from falling rocks dislodged from above.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/6-the-count-of-petals/sangrist":{"lang":["Common","Necril"],"notes":{"hp":"void healing","per":"blood scent 30 feet"},"flavor":"<p>Sangrists are born from the bodies of those who bled out completely, often when they are left unburied without proper rites in places tainted by void energy, though the strong presence of void energy is not a requirement. Some victims who rise as sangrists suffered an accident or attack in a remote region and were never found and properly laid to rest, be it a slip of a blade when cleaning game or being gored by a wild hog.</p>\n<p>After rising as undead, these tragic creatures are drawn to sites of past carnage where the scent of blood and death lingers. They are commonly found in the war-torn lands of Lastwall, the haunted ruins of Ustalav, and the cursed battlefields of Geb, embodying a relentless hunger for the life they lost.</p>\n<p>Sangrists rely on stealth and ambushes, becoming more active at night. During times of inactivity, they fall into a strange stupor. In this state, they rock back and forth and frequently twitch, sometimes emitting a haunting humming sound.</p>\n<p>Some sangrists are solitary hunters, often stalking a solitary target for hours if not days. Others form packs, especially if they shared a bond in life. These packs exhibit a chilling, coordinated cunning, reminiscent of wolves on the prowl. Sangrists are patient hunters, sometimes tracking their victims for miles or observing them for hours before making a move. They target the weak and isolated, picking off those they can easily wound so that they bleed out even if they manage to outrun the sangrists. Retaining only a shadow of their former selves, sangrists rarely utter more than single-word sentences, usually to express their hatred for the living, their unending hunger, or where victims can be found.</p>\n<p>Although sangrists can't drink blood or gain nourishment from it, they sometimes pool blood in their cupped hands and pour it on their faces and bodies. They may also lie down next to the body of a slain enemy. This macabre ritual seems to be a desperate attempt to reconnect with the life they once had. These strange proclivities toward blood, nighttime activity, and the yearning for some semblance of their previous lives lead some sangrists to associate with vampires. Vampires generally don't find sangrists distasteful and find the undead creatures easy to manipulate into serving them as guardians and attendants.</p><h2>Taste for Blood</h2><p>These rare creatures have been mistaken for either feral vampires or blood obsessed zombies by soldiers and adventurers who don't have a firm understanding of the myriad forms undeath can come in. Those familiar with sangrists and who aim to hunt them often set out blood, frequently pig blood, as a lure.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Blood Scent","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A sangrist can detect creatures taking persistent bleed damage as an imprecise sense at the listed range.</p>"},{"n":"Blood Vigor","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>While adjacent to a creature taking persistent bleed damage, the sangrist gains fast healing 3 and is Quickened until the start of its next turn. The sangrist can use its extra action only to Step or Stride.</p>"},{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Bloodletter","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A sangrist intuitively exacerbates open cuts on living creatures with its attacks. The sangrint's claw Strikes deal an additional 1 precision damage to living creatures taking persistent bleed damage.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/1-the-path-river-peeler/shiverscale-kobold":{"lang":["Common","Sakvroth"],"gear":["Crossbow","Shortsword","Leather Armor"],"abilities":[{"n":"Scamper","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The kobold warrior is adjacent to at least one enemy</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The kobold warrior Strides up to their Speed plus 5 feet and gains a +2 circumstance bonus to AC against reactions triggered by this movement. They must end this movement in a space that's not adjacent to any enemy.</p>"},{"n":"Sneak Attack","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The Shiverscale kobold deals an extra 1d4 precision damage to Off-Guard creatures.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/6-the-count-of-petals/spear-launcher-troubles-in-grayce":{"description":"<p>A wall socket loaded with a spear connects to a floor tile in one 5-foot square.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 18 Thievery (trained) on the floor tile or wall socket</p>","reset":"<p>The launchers require manual reload.</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(trained)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Spear","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["attack"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> Pressure is applied to the floor tile.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The trap shoots a spear, making a Strike against the creature or object on the floor tile.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/1-the-path-river-peeler/spiked-walls":{"description":"<p>A low trip wire connects to a lattice panel covered with wooden spikes.</p>","disable":"<p>Thievery (trained) to snip the trip wire or jam the panel</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Spiked Wall","a":"r","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature triggers the trip wire</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> A lattice panel lashed with wooden spikes swings shut across the passage. The triggering creature takes 3d6 piercing damage (DC 17 basic Reflex save). The panel provides lesser Cover and prevents movement along the passage. The panel can be pushed open like a door with an Interact action, but it swings shut again unless wedged open with a heavy object.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/5-sweet-tooth/spitting-hot-candy-pot":{"description":"<p>A massive bronze cauldron of molten candy bubbles on the stove, spraying sticky-sweet hot caramel in all directions.</p>","disable":"<p>Breaking the pot disables it as soon as its rupture reaction is resolved.</p>","routine":"<p>(2 actions) On each of the pot's actions, it targets one creature hostile against Larmine within 30 feet of the pot, spitting a glob of hot molten candy that deals 1d8+4 fire damage; the target creature must attempt a DC 16 Reflex save.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The target is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The target takes half damage.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The target takes full damage and is Slowed 1 as the sticky molten candy glues them to the floor.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The target takes double damage, is Slowed 2, and takes 1d4 persistent fire damage as the hot candy sticks to their exposed skin.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Bubble Bubble","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["occult"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> Larmine rolls initiative</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> With a wave of her hand, Larmine instantly brings her candy pot to a violent boil. The pot blows its top and targets all creatures hostile to Larmine within 20 feet of it, dealing 2d8 fire damage with a DC 16 basic Reflex save. On a critical failure, the targeted creature also takes 1d4 persistent fire damage as the hot candy sticks to their exposed skin. The spitting hot candy pot then rolls initiative.</p>"},{"n":"Rupture","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The spitting hot candy pot Breaks</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The pot splits open, spilling its remaining contents in a 15-foot cone that deals 2d12 fire damage with a DC 20 Reflex save. The area covered by this reaction lingers as difficult terrain for the rest of the encounter.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The creature takes half damage.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature takes full damage and becomes tangled in the sticky mess of hot candy, which imposes a –10-foot circumstance penalty to its Speeds until it leaves the area of the rupture's effect. Until the creature leaves the area of effect, it also takes 1d4 persistent fire damage.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature takes double damage and is Immobilized (DC 18 to Escape), after which it takes a –10-foot circumstance penalty to its Speeds and 1d4 persistent fire damage as failure.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/5-sweet-tooth/sticky-toffee-hound":{"flavor":"<p>Sweet hags have an affinity for baking and take delight in corrupting all that is sweet and scrumptious. Like most bakers, each hag cultivates a personal collection of favorite recipes they keep coming back to. For Larmine, the hag occupying Dalmira's Bakery, the most loyal of her specialties are her sticky toffee hounds.</p>\n<p>Though modeled after noble hunting hounds, there's only so much magic can do to hold sponge cake and toffee sauce in a roughly canine shape. These sticky-sweet creations therefore have all the loyalty of a dog and all the versatility of an ooze, to the point that they practically hold themselves together through sheer force of devotion to their creator. However, the saccharine hounds have little awareness of their creator's mortality; were Larmine ever to die, Grayce would be serenaded nightly for weeks by the burbling howls of her mourning pets. In fact, the toffee oozes might eventually become so desperate for attention that they latch onto defenseless civilians, even suffocating their new (and unwitting) owners in a desperate quest for affection.</p>\n<p>A sticky toffee hound is an irresistible trap for insects, whose congregation can give away the ooze's presence. The hound's body is ultimately lethal, though, trapping animals in its gooey hide like mosquitoes in amber.</p><h2>Half Baked Creations</h2><p>Sticky toffee hounds aren't the only option for enterprising sweet hags who want to make companions. Creatures like this can be in any form and shape, but animal shapes and behaviors are the most often mimicked. Sweet hags can also make companions out of breads, doughs, and other confections, lending to a buffet of dangerous and potentially tasty allies.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Motion Sense","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A sticky toffee hound can sense nearby creatures through vibration and air movement.</p>"},{"n":"Overcooked","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The sticky toffee hound is reduced to 0 hit points by any method other than its water soluble weakness</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The sticky toffee hound explodes, dealing 1d4 bludgeoning plus 1d4 fire damage in a 15-foot emanation with a DC 15 basic Reflex save.</p>"},{"n":"Water Soluble","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When exposed to at least 1 bulk of water, or a spell or effect with the water trait (even one that normally doesn't do any damage), the sticky toffee hound takes 10 acid damage as the sugar that makes up their body melts away, in addition to any damage that would normally be applied.</p>"},{"n":"Sticky Paws","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A sweet residue cakes the tracks left by a sticky toffee hound. A creature Tracking the hound can roll a Cooking Lore or Crafting check instead of a Survival check. The creature can apply any modifiers to this check that involve identifying or preparing food, such as those granted by the Seasoned skill feat or the Supertaster general feat.</p>"},{"n":"Toffee Sauce","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A creature hit by the sticky toffee hound's headbutt Strike is splattered by the sticky toffee sauce that soaks the creature's body, becoming Clumsy 1 until the end of their next turn. On a critical hit, the creature instead becomes Clumsy 2.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/4-the-disgraced-dollmaker/studious-butler":{"lang":["one spoken by its creator (typically Common)"],"flavor":"<p>An automatic butler is a magical construct shaped like a wooden humanoid mannequin, usually tailored in a simple black suit and tie. It has only the barest hint of a face on its round head, though its jaw opens on a hinge to simulate regular mouth movements when it speaks. Its voice is often calm and measured, and it speaks quite politely. It follows the orders given to it by its creator (or anyone whom the creator has given permission to issue commands), which usually involves fetching items, keeping a room tidy, or ushering out guests. If unwanted guests become belligerent, an automatic butler can defend itself by battering intruders or using its innate magic to hurl objects with deadly force. Once a fight has ended, an automatic butler usually spends the next hour or so cleaning up the mess left behind. Automatic butlers are built on designs similar to soulbound dolls. Like other soulbound constructs, an automatic butler is animated by a fragment of the soul of a deceased mortal. This fragment can come from either a willing donor or an unwilling victim, who can resist the process with a successful Will save against the creator's Crafting DC. While resisting ruins the construct, the soul extraction process is often fatal, and the person dies regardless. Unlike with other soulbound constructs, the personality of the soul used to create an automatic butler is wholly suppressed. Instead, the automatic butler comports itself in the way a stereotypical servant would. Some automatic butlers are created with slightly different skill sets. They can be created to speak more languages beyond that of their creator, making them passable family diplomats who might welcome foreign guests. Some are trained to take care of tasks that require specialization, such as tutoring the youth of the household or tailoring the family's clothes. The sidebar covers some other options for a whole domestic staff of constructed servants.</p><h2>Automatic Cooks and Gardeners</h2><p>Should the designs for creating automatic butlers reach the wider world, someone would likely end up creating variations of the magical construct. These creatures would use the same basic statistics as an automatic butler, with a few minor changes. An automatic cook might have cleanse cuisine as an innate spell, a knife Strike, and the ability to shoot a jet of boiling hot water. An automatic gardener might have scatter scree and other spells with the earth or plant traits as innate spells, a hoe or shovel Strike, and the ability to pelt foes at range with seeds.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Soul Fragment","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Like other soulbound constructs, an automatic butler is animated by soul energy, though the donor soul's personality is suppressed in favor of a simple demeanor, either politely helpful or stoically pragmatic. It also has none of that creature's memories. This soul fragment removes the immunity to spirit most constructs have, but because the automatic butler's personality overrides that of the original creature, the butler does have some resistance to spirit damage.</p>"},{"n":"Right This Way","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"once per round","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per round</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The automatic butler attempts to usher a creature along while removing their coat. The automatic butler attempts an Athletics check to Reposition a Large or smaller creature within reach. On a success, the target is also Clumsy 1 (Clumsy 2 on a critical success) as the automatic butler pulls at some part of their clothing or gear. The target can remove this condition by taking an Interact action.</p>"},{"n":"Studious Strike","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"once per round","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per round</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The automatic butler learns from its mistakes. If the butler's last action was to attempt a fist Strike that missed (but not critically missed) its target, the butler makes a fist Strike against the same target. The target is Off-Guard to the attack, and on a hit, the butler deals an additional 1d6 precision damage.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/6-the-count-of-petals/the-count-of-petals":{"lang":["Common","Necril","Varisian"],"gear":["Rapier","Leather Armor"],"notes":{"hp":"coffin restoration, fast healing 7, void healing"},"abilities":[{"n":"Children of the Night","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["divine","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>The vampire's presence brings forth creatures of the night to do the master's bidding. These typically include Rat Swarms, Bat Swarms, and Wolves, but can include other creatures.</p>\n<p>The vampire can give telepathic orders to these creatures as long as they are within 100 feet, but they can't communicate back.</p>"},{"n":"Fast Healing 7","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A monster with this ability regains the given number of Hit Points each round at the beginning of its turn.</p></p>"},{"n":"Coffin Restoration","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["divine","void"],"f":"","d":"<p>Unlike other undead, a vampire isn't destroyed at 0 HP. Instead, it falls Unconscious. If its body rests in its coffin for 1 hour, the vampire gains 1 HP, after which its fast healing begins to function normally.</p>"},{"n":"Mist Escape","a":"f","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The Count of Petals is reduced to 0 HP</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The Count of Petals uses Turn to Mist. He can take move actions to move toward his coffin even though he's at 0 HP. While at 0 HP in this form, the Count is unaffected by further damage. He automatically returns to his corporeal form, Unconscious, if he reaches his coffin or after 2 hours, whichever comes first.</p>"},{"n":"Vampire Vulnerabilities","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>All vampires possess the following vulnerabilities.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Compulsions</strong> Vampires are creatures with strange and unknowable compulsions. A typical vampire can't voluntarily cross running water unless they're transported while they hide within their coffin, nor can they enter a private dwelling unless invited in by someone with the authority to do so. At your discretion, vampires might have different compulsions—a pirate vampire might not be able to set foot on solid ground without being invited, for example. The vampire can still be forced to do these things and might be able to overcome their compulsion just as they do their revulsion.</li>\n<li><strong>Revulsion</strong> A vampire can't voluntarily come within 10 feet of brandished garlic or a brandished religious symbol of a deity with a holy sanctification option. To brandish garlic or a religious symbol, a creature must Interact to do so, and it remains brandished for 1 round (similar to Raising a Shield). If the vampire involuntarily comes within 10 feet of an object of their revulsion, they gain the Fleeing condition, running from the object of their revulsion until they end an action beyond 10 feet. After 1 round of being exposed to the subject of their revulsion, a vampire can attempt a DC 25 Will save as a single action, which has the concentrate trait. On a success, they overcome their revulsions for 1d6 rounds (or 1 hour on a critical success).</li>\n<li><strong>Stake</strong> A wooden stake driven through a vampire's heart drops the vampire to 0 HP and prevents them from healing above 0 HP, even in their coffin. Staking a vampire requires 3 actions and works only if the vampire is Unconscious. If the stake is removed, the vampire can heal above 0 HP again, and if they're in their coffin, the 1-hour rest period begins once the stake is removed. If the vampire's head is severed and anointed with holy water while the stake is in place, the vampire is destroyed.</li>\n<li><strong>Sunlight</strong> If exposed to direct sunlight, a vampire immediately becomes Slowed 1. The slowed value increases by 1 each time the vampire ends their turn in sunlight, and the condition ends when they're no longer in sunlight. If the vampire loses all their actions in this way, they're destroyed. Due to their supernatural aversion to light, vampires don't cast shadows or show a reflection in mirrors\n</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Change Shape","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","divine","polymorph"],"f":"","d":"<p>The vampire transforms into one of its animal forms or back into its normal form. Most vampires can turn into a bat, but some can turn into a different creature, such as a rat or a wolf.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Giant Bat</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Senses</strong> echolocation 40 feet,</li>\n<li><strong>Speed</strong> 20 feet, fly 30 feet,</li>\n<li><strong>Melee</strong> fangs +15 <strong>Damage</strong> 1d8+9 piercing</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<hr>\n<p><p>The monster changes its shape indefinitely. It can use this action again to return to its natural shape or adopt a new shape. Unless otherwise noted, a monster cannot use Change Shape to appear as a specific individual. Using Change Shape counts as creating a disguise for the Impersonate use of Deception. The monster's transformation automatically defeats Perception DCs to determine whether the creature is a member of the ancestry or creature type into which it transformed, and it gains a +4 status bonus to its Deception DC to prevent others from seeing through its disguise. Change Shape abilities specify what shapes the monster can adopt. The monster doesn't gain any special abilities of the new shape, only its physical form. For example, in each shape, it replaces its normal Speeds and Strikes, and might potentially change its senses or size. Any changes are listed in its stat block.</p></p>"},{"n":"Create Servitor","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["divine","downtime"],"f":"","d":"<p>If a creature dies after being reduced to 0 HP by Drink Blood, the Count of Petals can turn this victim into a vampire servitor by donating some of his own blood to the victim and burying them in earth for 3 nights. A vampiric servitor is compelled to obey its creator, but if the Count of Petals controls too many vampiric servitors at once (as determined by the GM), or if the servitor is a higher level than the vampire that created it, strong-willed servitors can free themselves by succeeding at a Will saving throw against the vampire's Will DC.</p>"},{"n":"Dominate","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["divine","incapacitation","mental","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p>The Count of Petals can cast Dominate at will as a divine innate spell. Casting it requires staring into the target's eyes, giving the spell the visual trait. The save DC for this ability is 22, and a creature that succeeds is temporarily immune to his Dominate for 24 hours. Fully destroying the Count of Petals ends the domination, but merely reducing the vampire to 0 HP is insufficient to break the spell.</p>"},{"n":"Drink Blood","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["divine"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirement</strong> A Grabbed, Paralyzed, Restrained, Unconscious, or willing creature is within the vampire's reach.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The vampire sinks its fangs into that creature to drink its blood. This requires an Athletics vs. Fortitude DC check against the victim's Fortitude DC if the victim is grabbed and is automatic for any of the other conditions.</p>\n<p>The victim is Drained 2 and the vampire regains 10 HP, gaining any excess HP as temporary Hit Points. Drinking Blood from a creature that's already drained doesn't restore any HP to the vampire but increases the victim's drained value by 1, killing the victim when it reaches drained 5. A vampire can also consume blood that's been emptied into a vessel for sustenance, but it gains no HP from doing so.</p>\n<p>A victim's drained condition decreases by 1 per week. A blood transfusion, which requires a DC 20 Medicine check and sufficient blood or a blood donor, reduces the drain by 1 after 10 minutes.</p>"},{"n":"Soporific Stench","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["aura","incapacitation","olfactory"],"f":"","d":"<p>10 feet. For 3 rounds, the Count of Petals reeks of the perfumed sweetness of spoiled flowers, which makes others sluggish and confused. A creature entering the aura or starting its turn in the aura must attempt a DC 22 Fortitude save.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is Stupefied 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is Stupefied 2</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is Stupefied 3 and Slowed 1.</p>"},{"n":"Turn to Mist","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["air","concentrate","divine","polymorph"],"f":"","d":"<p>The Count of Petals turns into a cloud of vapor, as the Vapor Form spell, or back to his normal form. He loses fast healing while turned to mist. The count can remain in this form indefinitely.</p>"},{"n":"Grab","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Grab and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/2-sabotage/the-dig-beast":{"abilities":[{"n":"Wild Curse","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["curse","magical"],"f":"","d":"<p>The Dig Beast is loaded with cursed energy but also is hard to control. At the start of its turn, roll 1d6. On a 1–2 it is Slowed 1 until end of its turn, on a 3–4 it is neither slowed nor Quickened (unless from another effect), and on a 5–6 it is quickened until the end of its turn.</p>"},{"n":"Crashing Ram","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The Dig Beast rushes at a target and crashes into it. The Dig Beast strides twice and performs a shovel teeth attack against each adjacent target. These attacks count against its multiple attack penalty, but the multiple attack penalty doesn't increase until after all the attacks.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/4-the-disgraced-dollmaker/trial-by-flame-trap":{"description":"<p>Nozzles connected to reservoirs of alchemist's fire are hidden just under the packed dirt of the floor, ready to spew burning jets when deployed.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 23 Thievery (trained) to disable the nozzles or DC 26 Arcana (expert) or DC 26 Occultism (expert) to disable the nearby magical sensor</p>","reset":"<p>The trap resets when an operator at the maze console in area <strong>A13</strong> pulls the associated lever. The trap can be reset only once per minute.</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(trained)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Burning Jets","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> An operator at the maze console in area <strong>A13</strong> pulls the associated lever</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Three hidden nozzles rise from the ground, and each makes a flame jet Strike against a creature in the area marked on the map. A creature can be hit more than once if there are fewer than three targets. On a critical hit, the target takes 1d10 persistent fire damage.</p>"}],"strikeNotes":{"Flame Jet":"<p>No multiple attack penalty</p>"}},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/4-the-disgraced-dollmaker/trial-by-ice-trap":{"description":"<p>A hidden vent in the ceiling is attached to a reservoir of freezing alchemical reagents that can create a bone-chilling cloud.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 23 Thievery (trained) to jam the vent closed or DC 26 Arcana (expert) or DC 26 Occultism (expert) to disable the nearby magical sensor</p>","reset":"<p>The trap resets when an operator at the maze console in area <strong>A13</strong> pulls the associated lever. The trap can be reset only once per minute.</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(trained)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Frozen Mist","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["alchemical","cold"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> An operator at the maze console in area <strong>A13</strong> pulls the associated lever</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> A vent opens in the ceiling, issuing forth a cloud of frigid fog that quickly fills the area marked on the map. Each creature in the area takes 6d8 cold damage (DC 26 basic Fortitude save). Creatures that fail the save also take 1d8 persistent cold damage (2d8 persistent cold damage on a critical failure). The area remains cold for the next minute, dealing 2d6 cold damage (DC 22 basic Fortitude save) to any creature who ends their turn in the area.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/4-the-disgraced-dollmaker/trial-by-poison-trap":{"description":"<p>A dart launcher embedded in the ceiling holds a cartridge of darts tipped with deadly poison.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 23 Thievery (trained) to disable the launcher or DC 26 Arcana (expert) or DC 26 Occultism (expert) to disable the nearby magical sensor</p>","reset":"<p>The trap resets when an operator at the maze console in area <strong>A13</strong> pulls the associated lever. The trap can be reset only once per minute.</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(trained)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Toxic Barrage","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> An operator at the maze console in area <strong>A13</strong> pulls the associated lever</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> A dart launcher emerges from the ceiling and makes a poison dart Strike against two different creatures in the area marked on the map. A single creature can't be targeted more than once.</p>"}],"strikeNotes":{"Poison Dart":"<p>No multiple attack penalty</p>"}},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/2-sabotage/unlucky-collapsing-tunnel":{"description":"<p>At the unluckiest time, the shoddily supported tunnel wall collapses in on itself and floods the tunnel with dirt and stone.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 18 Nature check to counteract the aura of unluck that pervades the tunnels for 1 minute, or DC 20 Survival to support the tunnel ceiling properly without triggering a collapse</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(trained)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Collapse","a":"r","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature enters the tunnel</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The tunnel falls in, dealing 2d10 untyped damage to each creature in the area (DC 20 basic Reflex save). Creatures in the area must spend at least 5 minutes digging themselves out to continue (DC 20 Survival halves this time).</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/3-they-hunger-below/unstable-floor":{"description":"<p>An unstable section of limestone crumbles under the weight of anyone who steps on it.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 20 Engineering lore, DC 20 Mining lore, or DC 23 Survival to determine the exact extent of the unstable ground and to optionally cause it to collapse safely into area <strong>A2</strong> below</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(trained)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Deeper into the Darkness","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A Small or larger creature walks onto the weak limestone</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The ground collapses, and the triggering creature falls 15 feet into area <strong>A2</strong> below. The creature can try to Grab an edge to avoid falling, otherwise the fall inflicts 7 bludgeoning damage plus an additional 4d6+3 bludgeoning damage (DC 23 basic Reflex save) from falling rubble.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/1-the-path-river-peeler/unstable-overlook":{"description":"<p>A ledge overlooks a yawning chasm.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 20 Acrobatics to tread lightly across the overlook onto more stable ground. Permanently disabling the hazard requires several hours of work and a successful DC 23 Crafting check (trained).</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Collapse","a":"r","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A Small or larger creature moves onto the weakened section of the overlook</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The ledge collapses. Any creature on the ledge that fails to Grab an edge plummets 40 feet into the chasm below.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/4-the-disgraced-dollmaker/wall-blades-trap":{"description":"<p>A series of rotating, serrated blades attached to long poles routinely slice out of grooves carved into the stone walls.</p>","disable":"<p>DC 20 Thievery (trained) to disengage the mechanisms that operate the blades in one wall</p>","reset":"<p>The trap deactivates when the room is empty and resets after 1 minute.</p>","routine":"<p>(3 actions) For each wall in which the mechanisms have been disabled or destroyed, the hazard has 1 fewer action. On each action, the hazard makes a serrated blade Strike against one creature within 10 feet of the west, east, or south wall. Each set of wall blades acts once per turn.</p>","stealthNote":"<p>(trained)</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Slide and Dice","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature is within 10 feet of a trapped wall</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The hazard makes a serrated blade Strike against all creatures within 10 feet of the west, east, or south wall; these Strikes don't take a multiple attack penalty. The hazard then rolls initiative.</p>"}],"strikeNotes":{"Serrated Blade":"<p>No multiple attack penalty</p>"}},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/4-the-disgraced-dollmaker/whimsical-butler":{"lang":["one spoken by its creator (typically Common)"],"flavor":"<p>An automatic butler is a magical construct shaped like a wooden humanoid mannequin, usually tailored in a simple black suit and tie. It has only the barest hint of a face on its round head, though its jaw opens on a hinge to simulate regular mouth movements when it speaks. Its voice is often calm and measured, and it speaks quite politely. It follows the orders given to it by its creator (or anyone whom the creator has given permission to issue commands), which usually involves fetching items, keeping a room tidy, or ushering out guests. If unwanted guests become belligerent, an automatic butler can defend itself by battering intruders or using its innate magic to hurl objects with deadly force. Once a fight has ended, an automatic butler usually spends the next hour or so cleaning up the mess left behind. Automatic butlers are built on designs similar to soulbound dolls. Like other soulbound constructs, an automatic butler is animated by a fragment of the soul of a deceased mortal. This fragment can come from either a willing donor or an unwilling victim, who can resist the process with a successful Will save against the creator's Crafting DC. While resisting ruins the construct, the soul extraction process is often fatal, and the person dies regardless. Unlike with other soulbound constructs, the personality of the soul used to create an automatic butler is wholly suppressed. Instead, the automatic butler comports itself in the way a stereotypical servant would. Some automatic butlers are created with slightly different skill sets. They can be created to speak more languages beyond that of their creator, making them passable family diplomats who might welcome foreign guests. Some are trained to take care of tasks that require specialization, such as tutoring the youth of the household or tailoring the family's clothes. The sidebar covers some other options for a whole domestic staff of constructed servants.</p><h2>Automatic Cooks and Gardeners</h2><p>Should the designs for creating automatic butlers reach the wider world, someone would likely end up creating variations of the magical construct. These creatures would use the same basic statistics as an automatic butler, with a few minor changes. An automatic cook might have cleanse cuisine as an innate spell, a knife Strike, and the ability to shoot a jet of boiling hot water. An automatic gardener might have scatter scree and other spells with the earth or plant traits as innate spells, a hoe or shovel Strike, and the ability to pelt foes at range with seeds.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Soul Fragment","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Like other soulbound constructs, an automatic butler is animated by soul energy, though the donor soul's personality is suppressed in favor of a simple demeanor, either politely helpful or stoically pragmatic. It also has none of that creature's memories. This soul fragment removes the immunity to spirit most constructs have, but because the automatic butler's personality overrides that of the original creature, the butler does have some resistance to spirit damage.</p>"},{"n":"Right This Way","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"once per round","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per round</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The automatic butler attempts to usher a creature along while removing their coat. The automatic butler attempts an Athletics check to Reposition a Large or smaller creature within reach. On a success, the target is also Clumsy 1 (Clumsy 2 on a critical success) as the automatic butler pulls at some part of their clothing or gear. The target can remove this condition by taking an Interact action.</p>"},{"n":"Whimsical Twirl","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"once per round","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per round</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The automatic butler Strides up to its speed without triggering reactions. Any creature adjacent to the butler at the end of this movement must succeed at a DC 20 Reflex save or be Off-Guard until the beginning of the butler's next turn.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/5-sweet-tooth/zini":{"lang":["Common","Goblin"],"gear":["Sling","Dogslicer","Leather Armor","Sling Bullets (10)"],"abilities":[{"n":"Goblin Scuttle","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A goblin ally ends a move action adjacent to the goblin.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The goblin commando Steps.</p>"},{"n":"Pent-Up Taunt","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","emotion","linguistic","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>Zini makes fun of either Aezar or Grizzle while she can observe them within 30 feet of her. She attempts a Deception vs. Will DC check with a +2 circumstance bonus against the creature's Will DC. Zini cannot use this ability again against that creature until she spends at least 10 minutes thinking about a worthy second insult.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> As success, but the target also takes 1d4+2 mental damage.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The target is Off-Guard against the next attack an ally attempts against the target before the beginning of Zini's next turn.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The target is unaffected.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/1-the-path-river-peeler/zoti":{"lang":["Common","Sakvroth"],"gear":["Spear","Steel Shield","Leather Armor"],"notes":{"ac":"20 with shield raised"},"abilities":[{"n":"Raise a Shield","a":"1","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> You are wielding a shield.</p>\n<hr>\n<p>You position your shield to protect yourself. When you have Raised a Shield, you gain its listed circumstance bonus to AC. Your shield remains raised until the start of your next turn.</p>"},{"n":"Shield Block","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> The monster has its shield raised and takes damage from a physical attack.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster snaps its shield into place to deflect a blow. The shield prevents the monster from taking an amount of damage up to the shield's Hardness. The monster and the shield each take any remaining damage, possibly breaking or destroying the shield.</p></p>"},{"n":"Scamper","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The kobold warrior is adjacent to at least one enemy</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The kobold warrior Strides up to their Speed plus 5 feet and gains a +2 circumstance bonus to AC against reactions triggered by this movement. They must end this movement in a space that's not adjacent to any enemy.</p>"},{"n":"Sneak Attack","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Zotri deals an extra 1d4 precision damage to Off-Guard creatures.</p>"}]},"troubles-in-grayce-bestiary/1-the-path-river-peeler/zurkurk":{"lang":["Common","Petran","Sakvroth"],"gear":["Staff"],"focus":1,"flavor":"<p>Kobold cavern mages are born more than trained, hatching from eggs that absorbed particularly large amounts of primal earth energy. Although there are many elementals and even natural terrain features that can give rise to cavern mages, most are related to the cavern-dwelling earth nymphs known as lampads. These lonely and capricious fey are as enthusiastic to receive the company of a kobold tribe as the kobolds are for the protection they receive in return.</p>\n<hr>\n<p>Kobolds are small reptilian humanoids. They lurk in dark spaces, usually tunnels and mines beneath the earth, in either warrens of their own design or complexes discovered and colonized after the original builders have moved on. Though kobolds are far more pragmatic than courageous, they use every inch of their cunning to even the playing field between themselves and other, stronger creatures. They attack from the darkness and at range, and kobold artificers and engineers master the art of simple but effective traps, which they use to protect their lairs. Kobolds are skilled at working together by necessity, and they often set up ambushes or hit-and-run assaults that allow them to do the most damage possible without being harmed in return.</p>\n<p>Kobolds are diligent and hardworking creatures. While some kobolds live in communal collectives that maintain neutral relations with the creatures around them, they can be easily swayed into serving malevolent powers or megalomaniacal leaders. This is in part due to kobolds' innate pragmatism, as they would rather concede to servitude than risk being killed, but it is also in part due to a reverence for the power they generally lack. Notably, kobold eggs left in the proximity of magical creatures or places tend to absorb similar traits from the exposure. The resulting physical changes mark the appearance of each tribe, but a few lucky kobolds are also born with magical power that reflects their tribe's patron.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Inspiring Display","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","emotion","linguistic","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> Zurkurk's previous action was to Cast a Spell</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Zurkurk uses his magical display to inspire another kobold within 30 feet. That kobold gains 4 temporary Hit Points that last until the start of Zurkurk's next turn.</p>\n<p>Effect: Inspiring Display</p>"},{"n":"Scamper","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The kobold cavern mage is adjacent to at least one enemy</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The kobold cavern mage Strides up to their Speed plus 5 feet and gains a +2 circumstance bonus to AC against reactions triggered by this movement. They must end this movement in a space that's not adjacent to any enemy.</p>"}]}});
